// CHTM Academy question bank, units 7 and 8. All questions are original management scenarios.
window.FRA = window.FRA || {};
FRA.questions = FRA.questions || [];
FRA.questions.push(
// u7l1
{id:"u7l1-1",t:"u7l1",q:"Four technicians have asked for service schools and your education line holds $21,000. One of the four is a refresher an accreditation checklist requires. How should the four be ranked?",a:["By the seniority of the technician asking, which is the fairest method","The required refresher first and without being scored on return at all, then single points of failure, then the shortest paybacks","Strictly by payback period, shortest first, with the required refresher scored on its return like any other course and dropped if it does not pay back","By the size of the fleet each course covers"],c:1,e:"Training a regulator, a license, or an accreditation checklist requires is not optional and is not justified on return. Everything else is ranked below it: first the courses that remove a single point of failure, then the shortest paybacks, then items with no cash return that still move a reported number."},
{id:"u7l1-2",t:"u7l1",q:"A manager costs a two-week factory school as tuition of $9,500 plus $2,800 of travel and lodging and presents $12,300 as the first-year cost. What has been left out?",a:["The depreciation of the fourteen devices the training covers, spread across the two weeks the technician is away","Nothing, because only invoiced amounts belong in a cost justification","The productivity lost while the technician is away, which is 80 hours at the fully loaded rate","The cost of the test equipment already owned by the department, prorated to the hours it will be idle"],c:2,e:"Two weeks of a technician is a real cost even though no invoice arrives for it. At about $72 an hour fully loaded, 80 hours adds $5,760 and brings the honest first-year figure to $18,060."},
{id:"u7l1-3",t:"u7l1",q:"Finance declines a service school because in year one it barely breaks even. What is the BEST response?",a:["Resubmit the request with the travel and lodging costs removed so the first-year figure breaks even with margin to spare","Ask the clinical department to fund it from their own budget","Accept the decision and record that training was refused","Show the accumulated position at years four, five, and six, where in-house cost crosses under contract cost"],c:3,e:"Training is a one-time expense: high in year one and close to zero afterwards. A single-year view kills almost every school worth attending, so the argument is made on the accumulated savings several years out."},
{id:"u7l1-4",t:"u7l1",q:"A school will cost $18,060 in its first year and will avoid a net $25,480 every year afterwards. What is the payback period?",a:["About 8.5 months","About 1.4 years","About 2.1 years","About 14 months"],c:0,e:"Payback is first-year cost divided by the net annual saving, so 18,060 / 25,480 is 0.71 years, roughly 8.5 months. The five-year return on the same figures is about 605 percent."},
{id:"u7l1-5",t:"u7l1",q:"A training request arrives saying only that the hospital owns fourteen of the model and the technician would like to attend. What else does a needs analysis have to establish?",a:["Only the cost of the course and whether the budget can carry it","The competency gap, the failure and repeat-repair data, the operations problem it fixes, the customer and regulatory requirements, the resources needed afterwards, and the return","Whether the technician has attended a school in the last three years","The manufacturer's opinion of whether the training is necessary"],c:1,e:"Asset inventory is one input of nine. A request that answers only the inventory question is a wish; one that answers competency, failure data, operations, customer and regulatory requirements, technical requirements, resources, and cost against return is a plan."},

// u7l2
{id:"u7l2-1",t:"u7l2",q:"Six months after a $21,000 pump school your director asks what the hospital got, and you find that nobody recorded the fleet's repeat repairs or repair times before anyone traveled. What has that cost you?",a:["Nothing, since the current figures can be compared with the national average for the model and the vendor's published repair-time benchmarks","The warranty on the training provided","The ability to prove the result, because a baseline reconstructed afterwards is an argument rather than evidence","The competency records for the two technicians"],c:2,e:"Effectiveness is measured as a before-and-after on data you already collect, which means the baseline has to be captured before the first person leaves. Afterwards you can only estimate what the position used to be."},
{id:"u7l2-2",t:"u7l2",q:"After a technician school, repeat repairs, first-time fix, repair time, vendor labor, and documented competencies all improved, but calls coded as use error stayed at the same level. What does the flat measure tell you?",a:["The training failed and should not be repeated","The use-error codes are being applied incorrectly by the technicians, since a successful school should have moved every line on the table","Use error should be reclassified as an equipment failure","The next program belongs with the clinical users, because technician training was never going to change use error"],c:3,e:"The measure that does not move is often the most useful line on the table. Use error is a clinical-user problem, so the finding directs the next in-service to the unit and the shift rather than to the shop."},
{id:"u7l2-3",t:"u7l2",q:"Six technicians need the same skill. One seat at the factory costs $12,300, and on-site delivery for six costs $26,000. Which option is BEST and why?",a:["On-site delivery, because cost per trained technician is about $4,333 against $12,300","The factory seat, because its total cost of $12,300 is lower than $26,000 and the other five can shadow the attendee when he returns","The factory seat, because factory training is always superior","Neither, because six technicians cannot be released at once"],c:0,e:"Cost per trained technician is total program cost divided by the number who come out documented competent, and it decides how you buy a course once the needs analysis has decided whether to buy it. Ask whether the on-site class is the same one the maker's own service staff attend."},
{id:"u7l2-4",t:"u7l2",q:"Vendor labor on a trained fleet fell sharply, but the service contract was also renegotiated in the same period. How should the report handle it?",a:["Claim the whole reduction, since the training was the larger change","Name the contract change, separate the labor and parts effects you can, and compare the trained fleet against a comparable untrained fleet","Delay the report until the new contract has run three full years, so the renegotiation washes out of the figures and only the training effect remains","Report only the competency improvements and leave cost out"],c:1,e:"A saving that ignores what else changed will be dismantled by the first analyst who looks at it. Naming the confounders and using an untrained comparable fleet as a control is close to proof and costs nothing, because you already hold the data."},
{id:"u7l2-5",t:"u7l2",q:"A technician has returned from a school and been assessed competent on the model. Where does that result belong?",a:["In the employee's training record only","In the manufacturer's training records and the technician's personnel file, since the maker issued the certificate and the hospital holds the employee","In the employee's training record, the competency record against the model in the CMMS, and the department's annual education report","In the operating budget as a completed expense"],c:2,e:"The training file proves the course happened, the competency record against the model proves the person can do the work, and the annual report puts the result in front of the committee that already follows completion and repair-time trends."},

// u7l3
{id:"u7l3-1",t:"u7l3",q:"The purchase order for four new ultrasound systems was signed last month and you are now asking the manufacturer for service training and service manuals. Why is this difficult?",a:["Manufacturers are legally required to supply training only before a sale","The warranty has already started, so training cannot be delivered","Service training can only be purchased in the first year of ownership","The money has already moved, so there is no negotiating leverage left"],c:3,e:"There is no legal requirement for a manufacturer to sell service training to an equipment owner, so every term you want has to be agreed while the sale is still in doubt. After the purchase order is signed the leverage is gone."},
{id:"u7l3-2",t:"u7l3",q:"You have negotiated two service school seats with a new imaging system, but the maker's next class is nine months away and the warranty runs for twelve. Which contract term protects the hospital?",a:["A clause stating that training is delivered inside the warranty period, or the warranty is extended until it has been","A clause allowing the hospital to cancel the purchase and return the system at the maker's expense if the class slips past the warranty","A clause requiring the maker to provide a loaner system during the class","A clause converting the unused seats into a parts discount"],c:0,e:"A seat you cannot use is not a seat. Training is written as delivered within the warranty period, with the warranty extended if delivery slips, so the hospital never pays for coverage it could not benefit from."},
{id:"u7l3-3",t:"u7l3",q:"Three months after delivery, the manuals, service keys, and software promised in the terms and conditions have still not arrived. Which arrangement would have prevented this?",a:["A penalty clause calculated per day of delay against the list price of each manual, service key, and software license still outstanding","An agreement with materials management that payment can be withheld until the listed items are delivered","A letter of complaint to the manufacturer's regional manager","Adding the items to the next capital request"],c:1,e:"Invoices are normally payable once everything carrying a price has been signed for, and the items a manager cares about most often sit in the terms and conditions with no price attached. Withholding payment is the strongest tool available and the one most often left out."},
{id:"u7l3-4",t:"u7l3",q:"Your competency records show one technician signed off on the entire sterilizer fleet and nobody else. What is the MOST appropriate action?",a:["Record it as acceptable, since the fleet is fully covered","Move the sterilizers onto a vendor contract","Train a second technician on the fleet, because one trained person is a single point of failure and the record is also the succession plan","Ask the technician to write each procedure into the CMMS step by step so that any technician in the shop can follow them whenever he is unavailable"],c:2,e:"Every critical model needs at least two competent people. Totalling the columns of a cross-training matrix turns competency records into a risk register, and the single-holder rows are exactly the courses that belong in next year's training plan."},
{id:"u7l3-5",t:"u7l3",q:"An independent company offers training on a model for noticeably less than the manufacturer's own school. How should the manager judge it?",a:["Decline it, because only the manufacturer holds the current service documentation and firmware, so an independent class is teaching from old material","Accept it, because independent training is always more useful than factory training","Ask the manufacturer whether it approves of the provider","Ask managers whose technicians actually sat in the class, and judge it on content and satisfaction rather than on who is offering it"],c:3,e:"Independent schools are often cheaper and sometimes teach more, since they have no contract to protect. The test is the same either way: check with people whose staff attended rather than assuming the maker's class is automatically better or more cost effective."},

// u7l4
{id:"u7l4-1",t:"u7l4",q:"A surveyor asks a technician how he decontaminates a device before opening it. He answers correctly, but there is no record that he was ever trained. What is the position?",a:["The undocumented completion is the same finding as a missed class","The correct answer satisfies the requirement, since competence is what matters","The record can be created now and dated to when the training was given","Only the department manager's training records are reviewed at survey"],c:0,e:"Required training is two documents: the teaching and the proof. A completion nobody recorded cannot be distinguished from a class that never happened, which is why the signature is chased with the same energy as the attendance."},
{id:"u7l4-2",t:"u7l4",q:"Your training matrix shows one technician signed for the department policy manual in 2019, and the manual has been revised several times since. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Note it as a historical record and move on, since he signed the manual once and revisions are circulated by email to everyone in the shop","Have him re-read and re-sign the current manual, with the revision recorded next to the signature","Issue a written warning for failing to keep current","Replace the manual with a shorter version everyone will read"],c:1,e:"A signature from 2019 means the technician has been working from a document the department no longer follows. The manual is re-signed whenever it is materially revised, and the revision number is recorded so the record says which version was agreed to."},
{id:"u7l4-3",t:"u7l4",q:"Two technicians on your matrix have no radiation safety training recorded because neither enters controlled areas. Is this a problem?",a:["Yes, every member of the department needs every safety topic, because the matrix is only defensible at survey when no cell is empty for anyone","Yes, because a surveyor will read the blanks as missed training","No, role-based topics apply to the people in that role, so the gaps are correct and should be marked as such","No, because radiation safety is owned by another department"],c:2,e:"General topics apply to everybody, including the manager and the twenty-year veteran. Role-based topics apply to the people in the role, and the matrix should show them as not applicable rather than blank so the record explains itself."},
{id:"u7l4-4",t:"u7l4",q:"Satisfaction scores for your department show technical competence at 4.6 while communication and follow-up have fallen to 3.2. What training does that point at?",a:["More model-specific technical training on the fleets those units hold","Nothing, since the overall score remains acceptable","A refresher on work order documentation standards","Soft-skills training in communication and customer service, bought deliberately and reinforced in shop meetings"],c:3,e:"The data says the repairs are good and the explaining is not. Soft skills can be taught, and they travel further than a model-specific class because they apply to every call a technician takes rather than to one fleet."},
{id:"u7l4-5",t:"u7l4",q:"A required safety topic is listed as annual. A technician completed it on 10 March last year. By when must it be completed again?",a:["Within twelve months of 10 March last year","By 31 December of the current calendar year","By the end of the quarter in which it was completed","Whenever the class is next offered by the hospital"],c:0,e:"Annual means inside twelve months of the last completion, measured from the date the person completed it rather than from the date a class happened to be offered or from the end of the calendar year."},

// u7l5
{id:"u7l5-1",t:"u7l5",q:"A new technician starts on Monday and nobody has written an orientation plan. What is the real risk?",a:["She will be slower than expected for the first month","She will learn by accident from whoever sits nearest, including the habits you have been trying to end","The hospital's own orientation program will be duplicated, wasting a week of her paid time on content human resources already delivers","Her probationary review will have to be delayed"],c:1,e:"Orientation is the cheapest quality improvement a manager gets to make and the window closes fast. Without a plan the department teaches the new hire by accident, and the practices it passes on are whatever is nearest rather than whatever is right."},
{id:"u7l5-2",t:"u7l5",q:"The shop is two people short and your supervisor wants to send a new hire in her second week to a ventilator call on her own. What is the correct decision?",a:["Allow it, because a ventilator call cannot wait and she has been observed working well","Allow it if she telephones the supervisor before returning the device to service, since a verbal check by a qualified person stands in for the sign-off","Refuse, because nobody works unsupervised on a device before the dated competency sign-off for that device exists","Allow it and document the competency afterwards based on how the call went"],c:2,e:"Initial competency is a dated document naming the model or skill, the method, and the assessor, and it exists before unsupervised work on that device. Short staffing is a reason to reassign the call, never a reason to close a sign-off early."},
{id:"u7l5-3",t:"u7l5",q:"What should be true about a new technician at the ninety-day milestone of a properly written orientation plan?",a:["She has completed facility orientation and read the procedure manual","She has taken her first week of on-call","She has been signed off on every model the department services","The competency checklist for her assigned scope is complete, the probationary review has been held with nothing new in it, and her goals and development plan are agreed"],c:3,e:"Day 30 is supervised work with the basic sign-offs complete, day 60 is the routine list alone on signed-off models, and day 90 is a complete checklist, the probationary review, and the first goals and development plan."},
{id:"u7l5-4",t:"u7l5",q:"You have told an experienced technician that he is the mentor for a new hire, but no hours were set aside and no goal was written. What usually happens?",a:["Mentoring becomes an invitation to ask if she gets stuck, which produces a technician who does not ask","The new hire progresses faster because she is not being watched, and an experienced technician's example teaches more than scheduled hours would","The mentor refuses the assignment","The mentor's own productivity improves"],c:0,e:"A mentorship that is assumed rather than scheduled quietly disappears. Name one mentor, write the goal for the ninety days, protect the hours, and choose for teaching ability and the habits you want copied rather than for years of service."},
{id:"u7l5-5",t:"u7l5",q:"At a probationary review you intend to raise a documentation problem that first appeared in week five and has never been mentioned. What does that reveal?",a:["That the probationary period is too short to judge performance, and it should be extended by ninety days so the pattern can be confirmed","That feedback was not given when it happened, so the review is being used to deliver news it should only be confirming","That the documentation standard needs to be rewritten","That the mentor should have handled it without involving the manager"],c:1,e:"A problem in week five was a conversation in week five and a dated line in the manager's notes in week five. The probationary review confirms what is already known, agrees the first goals, and starts the development plan."},

// u7l6
{id:"u7l6-1",t:"u7l6",q:"One pump model generated thirty-one calls in four months and twenty-two closed as cannot duplicate. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Replace the fleet, since the devices are clearly unreliable","Hold a hospital-wide in-service on infusion pumps for every shift at once, because thirty-one calls in four months is already a clear operator problem","Check that the codes are being applied consistently, then trend the calls by department, model, and shift before planning any training","Report the model to the manufacturer as defective"],c:2,e:"Use error and cannot duplicate are identified at the time of the call and then trended for patterns. Confirm the coding first, because if one technician codes everything cannot duplicate the trend is measuring documentation habits rather than clinical practice."},
{id:"u7l6-2",t:"u7l6",q:"Fourteen of a unit's twenty-two cannot-duplicate calls came from the night shift. A daytime in-service was held and the calls continued. Why?",a:["The in-service content was wrong for that model, because a session built on the manufacturer's generic slides does not cover the unit's own presets","The device needed a software update rather than training","Night staff are less receptive to education","Operator training has to run on every shift that touches the device, and the shift carrying the problem was never reached"],c:3,e:"Shift is usually the most useful cut of a failure trend and the one most often skipped. Sessions are scheduled on every shift, including nights and weekends, with attendance recorded by name and the staff who missed it booked into the next one."},
{id:"u7l6-3",t:"u7l6",q:"A new drug library was loaded onto the pump fleet in June and calls from one clinic rose immediately afterwards. Does this require operator training?",a:["Yes, a database or configuration change teaches old users a new device and is a training event","No, because a software change is a technical matter for the department","No, because the devices themselves were not changed","Yes, but only for staff hired after the change was made"],c:0,e:"New equipment, a new clinical application on an existing device, new or rotating staff, and any database or configuration change are all training events. A new drug library, preset, or alarm default changes the device the user meets even though nothing was replaced."},
{id:"u7l6-4",t:"u7l6",q:"You have the trend data showing a clear operator problem on one unit. Who should deliver the training?",a:["The department manager, since the data belongs to the department and the manager is the only person who can explain the trend behind the session","The unit's clinical educator with the manufacturer's clinical specialist, while the department supplies the data, the device, and the dates","The technician who closed most of the calls","The unit's charge nurse, without outside involvement"],c:1,e:"The manager facilitates rather than teaches. Clinical educators and vendor specialists carry credibility with the staff and the authority to pull people off the floor, and the department brings the pattern, the device knowledge, and the record of attendance."},
{id:"u7l6-5",t:"u7l6",q:"An in-service aimed at a use-error pattern has been delivered on every shift. How does the manager establish whether it worked?",a:["By asking the unit manager whether the staff found it useful and whether the nurses who attended now feel confident programming the device","By reviewing the attendance sheets for completeness","By counting the same code on the same model in the same units for the next three months against the original period","By repeating the in-service quarterly regardless of the result"],c:2,e:"Follow-up is part of the program, not an optional extra. Calls that fall and stay down close the item, calls that fall on days but hold on nights name the next session, and calls that do not move at all mean it was probably never use error."},

// u7l7
{id:"u7l7-1",t:"u7l7",q:"The only technician who knew the service passwords for the sterilizer fleet has retired, and nobody can get into the machines. What would have prevented this?",a:["A requirement in the job description that technicians hand over their notebooks and passwords to the manager on their last day of employment","Storing the passwords with the manufacturer's regional office","A service contract covering the fleet","An access register with one named owner, role-based vendor accounts, a review date, and retrieval tested by someone who did not set it up"],c:3,e:"The failure mode is always that one person held the knowledge and left. Access that depends on a single person's memory is not managed access, and the test is whether a technician who did not set it up can retrieve it in minutes."},
{id:"u7l7-2",t:"u7l7",q:"A new analyzer has been installed and accepted, but the service manuals and software promised in the agreement have not arrived and the invoice is due. What is the MOST appropriate action?",a:["Ask materials management to hold payment until the listed items are delivered, as the agreement provides","Pay the invoice on time to protect the relationship, and pursue the manuals and software separately through the sales representative","Accept the device without the materials and order them as a separate purchase","Return the analyzer to the manufacturer"],c:0,e:"Ask in writing at purchase for the manuals, passwords and keys, software and firmware updates, and technical support the department will need for as long as the hospital keeps the device, and arrange that payment can be held until they arrive. Where the state has adopted the healthcare facilities code, most of that list is already required."},
{id:"u7l7-3",t:"u7l7",q:"Your technicians need to agree which disinfectants may be used on a device fleet and how units must decontaminate items before the shop opens them. Which partner owns that conversation?",a:["Facilities management","Infection prevention","Materials management","Information technology"],c:1,e:"Infection prevention settles cleaning and disinfection between patients, what must be decontaminated before service, which agents damage which materials, and how reprocessing devices are validated and monitored."},
{id:"u7l7-4",t:"u7l7",q:"A planned power shutdown in a procedure wing needs to be coordinated, including which outlets sit on the emergency branch. Which partner does the manager work with?",a:["Materials management","Infection prevention","Facilities management","The accrediting organization"],c:2,e:"Facilities management owns normal and emergency power branches, water, medical gases, and ventilation, and is the partner for construction and utility interruption planning and for who responds to what overnight."},
{id:"u7l7-5",t:"u7l7",q:"A clinical unit keeps telephoning the department to ask how to run a cycle on a device it has had for years. What is the MOST likely cause?",a:["The device needs a software update","The device has been incorrectly configured by the department","The staff need technical rather than operator training","The unit's own operations manual is missing, and a lost manual becomes a service call"],c:3,e:"Operations manuals belong on the units as well as in the department, as controlled copies with an owner and a review date. A unit without its manual generates calls that look like equipment problems and are actually a library problem."},

// u8l1
{id:"u8l1-1",t:"u8l1",q:"Your strongest technician resigns and tells you in the exit conversation that he left because there was nowhere to go: one title, one grade, nothing above it. What management failure is that?",a:["No ladder of job descriptions and pay grades was ever written, so no promotion was possible","A recruitment failure, since he was overqualified when hired","A compensation failure, since the pay range for the single grade was too narrow to reward him for staying","A retention failure caused by the hospital's hiring freeze"],c:0,e:"Nobody can be promoted into a job that does not exist. If the department holds a single description, the only career move available to a good technician is to leave, and replacing him is estimated to cost more than 1.5 times his annual salary."},
{id:"u8l1-2",t:"u8l1",q:"A two-person department is told it does not need a full set of job descriptions because there is nowhere for anyone to be promoted to. What is the correct position?",a:["Two descriptions are sufficient until a third position is funded","A complete range of descriptions and steps is still needed, at least eight, because future positions are far easier to create as one coherent set","One description per person, reviewed when somebody is promoted","Descriptions should be written only when a vacancy is posted"],c:1,e:"Whatever the size of the department, the whole family of descriptions and grades is created in one sitting so that the steps line up and each is a genuine increase in duty and pay. Even a two-person department needs at least eight descriptions and steps on the books."},
{id:"u8l1-3",t:"u8l1",q:"A director argues that losing one technician costs the hospital little because the position can stay vacant for a few months. How should the manager answer?",a:["A vacancy saves money while it is unfilled, so the director is correct","The cost is limited to the recruitment advertising and the agency fee","Recruiting and training a replacement is estimated to exceed 1.5 times annual salary, before counting the equipment nobody can repair meanwhile","The cost cannot be estimated and should be left out of the discussion"],c:2,e:"Turnover in a labor-rich department is expensive in two ways: the recruiting and training cost, estimated above one and a half times annual salary, and the clinical disruption when nobody can repair highly technical equipment."},
{id:"u8l1-4",t:"u8l1",q:"Managers consistently rank what motivates employees as salary, bonuses, vacation, retirement, and benefits first. How do employees themselves rank it?",a:["The same way, with salary first, which is why pay drives retention","Training first, followed by salary and bonuses, because technicians want to stay current and then want to be paid for it","Vacation and retirement first, with salary in the middle","Interesting work, involvement in decisions, feedback, training, and respect first, with the money items last"],c:3,e:"The top five and bottom five are effectively reversed between managers and employees. Pay clearly below market will push someone out, but pay alone will not hold anyone in a job that is dull and in which nobody is consulted."},
{id:"u8l1-5",t:"u8l1",q:"A technician's performance has been poor for a year, nothing is in writing, and your director wants him terminated this week. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Involve human resources now and begin the documented path: the conversation, the written warning, then a performance improvement plan","Issue a written warning that cites the full year of poor performance and is dated to when the problems began, so the file catches up with the facts","Terminate as instructed and write the file up afterwards","Transfer him to another department to avoid the issue"],c:0,e:"Termination is the last step of a documented path, not the first response. Each step is recorded when it happens rather than reconstructed, human resources is involved from the beginning, and the same standard is applied to every employee in comparable circumstances."},

// u8l2
{id:"u8l2-1",t:"u8l2",q:"The annual evaluation is due on Friday and you are about to write down three criticisms the technician has never heard. What is wrong with that?",a:["The criticisms should be delivered verbally instead","Nothing should be new in an annual review, because providing feedback through the year is the manager's job","Three criticisms is too many for one review cycle","The criticisms should be raised with human resources first, so the review can cite them with HR's agreement already on record"],c:1,e:"The review is a summary of ground both people have covered together during the year. New information delivered there turns the meeting into a defense and makes any agreement about the next period almost impossible."},
{id:"u8l2-2",t:"u8l2",q:"A technician agreed a goal of documenting 85 percent of paid hours and delivered 79 percent. How should the evaluation record it?",a:["As unsatisfactory, since the goal was not achieved","As meeting the goal, since 79 percent is close to the target","As below the agreed measure by a stated amount, with a development step and a date attached","Leave it out, because the measure is unreliable"],c:2,e:"A rating of below means a missed measure named by amount against a defined target, and it never appears alone: it arrives with a development step and a date. That is what makes it a development conversation rather than an accusation."},
{id:"u8l2-3",t:"u8l2",q:"Every technician in a shop of eight has been rated as exceeding expectations for three years running. What is the consequence?",a:["Morale improves and retention follows, so the practice is sound","The evaluations become easier to defend if challenged","Human resources will raise the whole department's pay grade","The ratings carry no information and cannot support any pay or promotion decision"],c:3,e:"Meets has to be a respectable rating or the whole scale inflates. Where everybody exceeds, the document cannot justify a raise for the person who genuinely did more and cannot support a decision about the person who did less."},
{id:"u8l2-4",t:"u8l2",q:"A manager writes evaluations from memory in the last week of the period and the ratings track how the last six weeks happened to go. What is the fix?",a:["Collect dated, specific evidence across the whole period and pull the numbers from the system rather than remembering them","Ask each employee to write their own rating and supporting examples, since they remember their own year better than the manager does","Shorten the evaluation period to a quarter","Use a numerical scale with more levels"],c:0,e:"Recent weeks standing in for the year is one of the standard bias traps, along with one trait coloring everything, favoring people like you, and rating everyone in the middle. The defense against all of them is dated evidence plus figures taken from the records."},
{id:"u8l2-5",t:"u8l2",q:"Under observance of organizational values a manager has written that a technician has a great attitude. What should it say instead?",a:["A numerical score against each of the hospital's published values, averaged into one figure the technician can compare year on year","A named behavior with a date, such as reorganizing the pump store after an audit finding without being asked","The technician's own description of how he lives the values","Nothing, because values cannot be evaluated objectively"],c:1,e:"Values and engagement are real criteria and the easiest place for bias to hide, so they are evaluated the way everything else is: an observed behavior, on a date, that you or a documented complaint actually witnessed. Write the example, not the adjective."},

// u8l3
{id:"u8l3-1",t:"u8l3",q:"A technician tells you she wants to move into imaging, where you currently have one specialist and no backup. What is the BEST response?",a:["Tell her to apply when an imaging vacancy is posted","Promise her the next imaging course that becomes available and have the imaging specialist start showing her the fleet between calls","Write a development plan with the goal, the gap, the training, its cost and budget year, protected time, and a review date","Advise her to obtain the training at her own expense first"],c:2,e:"A development plan is what makes the answer real: dates on every step, a budget line for anything that costs money, and protected hours for the steps that do not. It also closes the single-holder gap on a fleet where a vendor visit is the only alternative."},
{id:"u8l3-2",t:"u8l3",q:"Your competency program uses self-assessment against a list of skills and models, and every technician has rated himself competent on everything. What must the manager do?",a:["Accept the results, since the technicians know their own abilities best","Replace the method with a written test for the whole department, because self-assessment can never be trusted as a competency method","Report the department as fully competent to the accreditor","Spot-check a sample of the claims personally, because self-assessment drifts optimistic without it"],c:3,e:"Self-assessment is fast, covers every skill and model, and produces a training plan for free, but it only stays honest when the manager spot-checks it. Most departments combine it with observation of critical devices and a review of repeat repairs."},
{id:"u8l3-3",t:"u8l3",q:"A review of repeat repairs shows several clustered on one technician's work. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Check whether a bad batch of parts or a troublesome device explains the cluster before concluding it is a training need","Place him on a performance improvement plan","Remove him from that equipment type","Report the repeat repairs to the manufacturer as an equipment reliability problem, since several in a row point at the device rather than the person"],c:0,e:"Repeat repairs are a legitimate competency indicator and also a symptom of bad parts, a bad model, or a technique issue specific to the device. Read the cause before you act, or you will train a technician for a parts problem."},
{id:"u8l3-4",t:"u8l3",q:"An administrator argues against paying for certification because certified technicians will simply leave for better jobs. How should the manager answer?",a:["Agree, and limit support to training that is specific to the hospital's own equipment","Point out that development binds people to an employer who pays fairly and takes the work seriously, and that certified staff are the simplest competence evidence at survey","Agree, but offer certification only to technicians with ten years of service","Suggest that technicians pay for certification themselves and be reimbursed if they stay"],c:1,e:"The worry gets the causation backwards. Certification is tied to defined steps in the career ladder and rewarded financially where policy allows, and the renewal cycles are tracked, because a lapsed credential takes a ladder step and a differential with it."},
{id:"u8l3-5",t:"u8l3",q:"You want to recommend a pay adjustment for a technician who has taken on the sterilizer fleet. What does human resources need from you?",a:["A statement that the technician is the best in the department","A copy of a competing job posting the technician has shown you, with the salary it advertises highlighted as the market rate","The grade, the comparable roles inside and outside the hospital, and market data, rather than an anecdote","A commitment that he will stay for two more years"],c:2,e:"Every recommendation arrives with the evidence that supports it: promotion needs the higher description and the competency records, pay needs the grade and the market comparison, discipline needs dated records, and accommodation needs the essential functions and never a diagnosis."},

// u8l4
{id:"u8l4-1",t:"u8l4",q:"A manager sets a technician the goal of doing better on documentation this year. Why will this fail?",a:["It is too easy and the technician will not take it seriously","It should have been set by human resources rather than the manager","Documentation is not a suitable subject for an individual goal, because it is a department measure that no single technician controls","It cannot be measured and has no date, so at the review the two of you can disagree in good faith about whether it happened"],c:3,e:"The test of a goal is whether you and the employee could disagree about whether it was met. Rewritten as 85 percent of paid hours documented against an asset or a department by the end of the second quarter, measured monthly, it can only be settled by a number."},
{id:"u8l4-2",t:"u8l4",q:"Your department's improvement plan was written by you alone and handed to the shop, and three months later nothing has changed. What was missed?",a:["Bringing the staff into setting the goals, because a plan the shop helped write gets implemented and one dropped on it gets resisted","Staff should have been given individual targets instead of a department plan, because people act on numbers with their own name against them","The plan should have been issued by the director rather than the manager","The plan should have been shorter"],c:0,e:"Individual goals ladder up from the department's goals for financial performance, completion, satisfaction, response time, repeat repairs, and use errors. Share those and explain why each matters, then set the individual goals with the person rather than for them."},
{id:"u8l4-3",t:"u8l4",q:"A manager gives the same written commendation to every member of the shop at the end of each quarter. What is the effect?",a:["Morale rises evenly across the department","Recognition becomes attendance, because it is generic and given to everyone equally","It satisfies the hospital's requirement for documented recognition and keeps the record of praise even across the shop","It substitutes effectively for a financial incentive"],c:1,e:"Recognition works when it is specific, prompt, and visible to peers: name what the person did and what it achieved. Technicians rank recognition and a real say in the department's direction above the money items, which is exactly why doing it badly is costly."},
{id:"u8l4-4",t:"u8l4",q:"Two technicians have been in open conflict for three weeks and the shop has taken sides. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Move one of them to a different shift","Hold a shop meeting and let everyone discuss the problem openly, since the whole shop has already taken sides","Hear each of them separately, on facts and behavior rather than on character","Document it and wait to see whether it resolves itself"],c:2,e:"Conflict is addressed early, separately, and on the facts, then checked against the policy and job descriptions so the resolution rests on something other than your preference. The agreement goes in writing to both with a date to check it held."},
{id:"u8l4-5",t:"u8l4",q:"A coaching conversation about poor findings in closed work orders went well, and the manager wrote nothing down. What has been lost?",a:["Nothing, because the behavior changed after the conversation","The technician's agreement to the improvement","The ability to use the conversation as a written warning, since a coaching note placed in the file is the first formal step of discipline","The only record that the conversation happened, which the manager will need if the behavior returns or an evaluation is challenged"],c:3,e:"One dated line in the manager's own notes is not a warning and is not in the file yet, but it is the difference between a documented pattern and an unsupported assertion. Improvement opportunities are written when they happen, in neutral language, with specifics."},

// u8l5
{id:"u8l5-1",t:"u8l5",q:"You are moving the department from paper work orders to entry on a handheld, and two influential technicians have already told the shop it will never work. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Explain the problem the change fixes in terms the shop cares about, and bring the people who will do the work into designing it","Announce the go-live date so nobody expects the decision to change","Discipline the two technicians for undermining a department decision","Run the change as a pilot on one bench without telling the shop it is permanent, so the two objectors have nothing to organize against"],c:0,e:"A change explained is implemented and a change announced is resisted. Why comes before what, the people who will do the work help design it, training happens before the switch rather than during it, and the result is measured and credited by name."},
{id:"u8l5-2",t:"u8l5",q:"During a change to new documentation tools, staff object that the new method will take them longer. What is the BEST response?",a:["Tell them the measurement will show otherwise once they are used to it","Acknowledge that it probably will at first, name the learning curve, and state the date by which you expect it to end","Remind them that the decision has already been made","Offer to keep the paper method available indefinitely for anyone who prefers it, since forcing a slower tool on experienced staff is unfair"],c:1,e:"Resistance usually has something real underneath it. This objection is normally true for a while, so the honest answer names the learning curve and commits to a date, which is more persuasive than denying the inconvenience."},
{id:"u8l5-3",t:"u8l5",q:"Human resources asks you to take part in an internal investigation into a technician's conduct. What is your role?",a:["To establish what happened and recommend the appropriate discipline","To represent the technician's interests, since he reports to you","To act as a factual witness for the organization, briefed by human resources and prepared from your own records","To gather statements from the rest of the department"],c:2,e:"The manager is a witness to facts and the keeper of the records, not the judge, the prosecutor, or the advocate. Be briefed by human resources and legal counsel first, read your own documentation so your account and the record agree, and bring dates and documents rather than impressions."},
{id:"u8l5-4",t:"u8l5",q:"While an investigation is open, the rest of the shop keeps asking you what is happening to their colleague. What is the correct response?",a:["Give a general outline so rumors do not take hold","Refer them to the technician under investigation","Explain the allegations so staff understand the seriousness","Say that you cannot discuss it, and discuss it with nobody outside the process"],c:3,e:"Confidentiality is part of the manager's role in a personnel matter, however much the department asks. A manager who discusses an open investigation in the shop has created a second problem on top of the first one."},
{id:"u8l5-5",t:"u8l5",q:"A year after an incident you are asked at a hearing what happened, and nothing was written at the time. What does this illustrate?",a:["That contemporaneous documentation is the whole defense: a note made on the day outweighs a better-worded one made later","That a manager should write the most detailed account he can now, dated to the day of the incident, so the hearing has a record to work from","That the hearing should be postponed until the record can be assembled","That another manager should give the evidence instead"],c:0,e:"Managers who document routinely walk into a hearing with the answer already on paper. Writing or backdating a record after the fact is one of the things never to do, alongside promising an outcome, retaliating, interviewing alone, and discussing the case with staff."},

// u8l6
{id:"u8l6-1",t:"u8l6",q:"Your on-call rotation has three qualified people, so each takes one week in three, and two of them have started applying elsewhere. What does the coverage model say?",a:["On-call pay should be increased to compensate for the frequency, because a differential is what the coverage model uses to hold people in a rotation","The rotation needs enough qualified people that nobody takes it worse than one week in four or five, which is a headcount question","On-call should be moved to a vendor contract","The rotation should be voluntary rather than assigned"],c:1,e:"Workload gives hours; coverage gives people. A rotation at one in three wears people out and they leave, a single person on a shift means no cover for leave or illness, and every critical skill needs a second holder, all of which drive headcount rather than hours."},
{id:"u8l6-2",t:"u8l6",q:"A hospital-employed technician earns just under $100,000 a year. What is the approximate fully loaded cost per hour to the hospital?",a:["About $50 an hour","About $95 an hour","About $72 an hour","About $120 an hour"],c:2,e:"Under $100,000 across 2,080 paid hours is under $50 an hour. Adding about 25 percent for benefits and about another 20 percent for supervision and unproductive time gives at most about $72, which sits below any outside hourly rate and below the labor inside most contracts."},
{id:"u8l6-3",t:"u8l6",q:"Two requests for an additional technician to catch up the scheduled maintenance backlog have been refused. What is the BEST way to make the case?",a:["Resubmit with the backlog figures presented as a patient safety risk","Request overtime funding instead of a position","Ask the clinical departments to write letters supporting the request","Justify the position against a quoted service contract you will decline, and leave deliberate slack in the new role to absorb the backlog"],c:3,e:"Prevented problems are invisible to the people who approve positions, so asking for staff to perform scheduled maintenance rarely succeeds. Replacing a quoted contract with cheaper in-house labor does succeed, and the slack in the new role is what absorbs the backlog."},
{id:"u8l6-4",t:"u8l6",q:"A manager proposes paying technicians a bonus for each work order they close. What is the likely result?",a:["Many small closed work orders and worse documentation, because the incentive rewards volume rather than the work","Higher productivity with no side effects","Better customer satisfaction scores across the department","Reduced overtime, since work is completed faster"],c:0,e:"Incentives that reward a count produce the count. Differentials for certification, on-call, shift, and specialty skills work because they pay for competence and coverage, and so does recognition with a genuine say in the department, which costs nothing."},
{id:"u8l6-5",t:"u8l6",q:"Two technicians doing comparable work at the same grade are paid noticeably differently and you cannot explain the gap on job-related grounds. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Adjust the lower salary at the next annual review cycle","Raise it with human resources with the criteria and the comparison, because wage-setting criteria must carry no influence of sex or race","Ask the two technicians not to discuss their pay with each other","Obtain a self-reported salary survey from a professional discussion list"],c:1,e:"Pay equity in law means the criteria an employer uses to set wages are neutral to sex and race, and managers review their staff's pay against that regularly. Market fairness is a separate question, answered by a survey human resources runs against matched job descriptions, not by self-reported numbers."},

// u8l7
{id:"u8l7-1",t:"u8l7",q:"A technician tells you that standing all day has become painful and asks for a stool at the bench. Which law governs this, and what is your first move?",a:["OSHA. Treat it as an ergonomic hazard, conduct an assessment of the bench, and record the stool as protective equipment the assessment called for","The FMLA. Advise him to apply for intermittent protected leave","The ADA. Discuss the essential functions of the job and what would let him perform them, and refer the request to human resources","The NLRA. Treat the request as a working-conditions matter for the group"],c:2,e:"Accommodation sits under the Americans with Disabilities Act, which protects a person with an impairment that substantially limits a major life activity, a record of one, or who is regarded as having one. Talk about essential functions, never ask for a diagnosis, and never decide alone that a job cannot be accommodated."},
{id:"u8l7-2",t:"u8l7",q:"A technician needs about six weeks away to care for a parent recovering from major surgery. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Approve the leave as unpaid and arrange coverage","Tell him to use his accrued vacation first and request leave afterwards","Ask for a letter from the parent's physician describing the condition and the expected recovery so you can judge whether six weeks is justified","Pass the request to human resources, who determine eligibility, then plan the coverage"],c:3,e:"The Family and Medical Leave Act gives eligible employees 12 workweeks of unpaid, job-protected leave in a 12-month period for a serious health condition of a spouse, child, or parent, with group health coverage continued and the job restored. Eligibility is determined by human resources, not by the manager."},
{id:"u8l7-3",t:"u8l7",q:"Two technicians are standing in the corridor openly comparing their pay and workload, and your supervisor wants to tell them that pay is not to be discussed at work. How should you respond?",a:["Stop the supervisor: employees acting together about the terms and conditions of their employment are engaged in activity protected by the NLRA","Allow the discussion but require it to happen away from clinical areas","Support the supervisor, because pay discussions damage morale","Report the conversation to human resources as a confidentiality breach"],c:0,e:"The National Labor Relations Act protects the right to organize, to bargain collectively, and to act together about pay and working conditions, whether or not a union is present. A manager does not question, discourage, watch, or retaliate against it, or let it affect an assignment or a review."},
{id:"u8l7-4",t:"u8l7",q:"A technician reports that the guard is missing from the shop bench grinder. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Add it to the next safety committee agenda for discussion","Make the hazard safe or take the grinder out of service now, report it through the safety program, and thank the person who raised it","Ask the technician to avoid using the grinder until a replacement guard is ordered","Investigate whether the technician removed the guard himself"],c:1,e:"The employer owes every worker a safe and healthful workplace, and an employee may report a hazard without retaliation. The sequence is assess, provide the protective equipment the assessment calls for, train, replace, and keep the records, and unsafe work does not continue while a fix is discussed."},
{id:"u8l7-5",t:"u8l7",q:"A technician tells you privately that a lead has repeatedly made remarks about her age and that she is afraid of losing shifts if she complains. What is the FIRST step?",a:["Speak to the lead informally and ask him to stop","Ask her to put the allegation in writing with dates and witnesses before anything is done, so the complaint can be investigated properly","Report it to human resources the same day and change nothing about her assignment or schedule in the meantime","Move her to a different shift so the two no longer work together"],c:2,e:"Age of 40 or older is one of the protected characteristics under the laws the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission enforces, and retaliation against someone who complains or takes part in an investigation is itself illegal. Report the same day, investigate through the process, and do not alter her assignment."}
);
